C MP-reorder
"Message passing with two INDEPENDENT writes on the writer (x then y) and two
 independent reads on the reader (y then x), no syntactic dependency linking the
 pairs. OHMM (Operational Happens-Before Model) permits the non-message-passing
 outcome r1=1 /\ r2=0: its abstract machine holds events in a GLOBAL EVENT BUFFER
 where independent (non-dependent) events may be reordered before they propagate
 into the global history-based memory, so the store to y can surface before the
 store to x. The OHMM paper states it 'is weaker than JMM for lockless programs
 ... such as the reordering of independent memory accesses that is not valid in
 JMM.' The Java Memory Model (JMM) FORBIDS this reordering: its commit-sequence
 causality rules do not justify it. Conversely JMM is famously over-permissive on
 causality and validates a different transformation set (Table 2 of the survey:
 JMM Elim = + + + -, OHMM Elim = + + + +), so each permits behaviour the other
 forbids -- they are incomparable. Neither verdict is machine-run: OHMM has no
 herd7/cat model and this reordering is OHMM-specific, not a JMM execution.
 Verdicts are CITED. See README."
{ int x=0; int y=0; }
P0 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y) {
  atomic_store_explicit(x,1,memory_order_relaxed);
  atomic_store_explicit(y,1,memory_order_relaxed);
}
P1 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y) {
  int r1 = atomic_load_explicit(y,memory_order_relaxed);
  int r2 = atomic_load_explicit(x,memory_order_relaxed);
}
exists (1:r1=1 /\ 1:r2=0)
